WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:18.272
RAE WANG: Ready?

00:00:18.272 --> 00:00:18.772
Hi.

00:00:18.772 --> 00:00:19.780
Good afternoon.

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:23.690
I am AUTHZ, also known
as authorization,

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:25.260
and this is my best buddy.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:29.520
JASON COLLINS: Hi, I'm AUTHN,
also known as authentication.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:33.910
I manage identities, your
users group sign in policies,

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:35.230
and directory sync.

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:36.980
RAE WANG: And I control
access management,

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:39.910
meaning who can do what
and which resources.

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:41.410
And together we're known as--

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:42.320
JASON COLLINS: I--

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:43.331
RAE WANG: AM.

00:00:43.331 --> 00:00:43.830
All right.

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:45.246
Let's do our real
[? ring ?] show.

00:00:49.195 --> 00:00:51.080
So, I am Rae Wang.

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:54.307
I'm a product manager on the
Google Cloud platform team.

00:00:54.307 --> 00:00:56.640
JASON COLLINS: And I'm Jason
Collins, a Cloud deployment

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:57.300
engineer.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:00.560
So we're here to
talk today about Iam,

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.420
how to do identity and access
management on Google Cloud

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:03.920
Platform.

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:11.130
So this is our agenda today.

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:15.020
I'm going to lay down some
foundational bits on identity.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:17.000
It's a little bit dry.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.750
The talk gets much better
when Rae gets back up on stage

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:23.590
to talk about access management
and some best practices.

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:26.720
And then the talk really
reaches a fever pitch

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:29.060
when we bring up a
couple of customers

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:31.560
who are doing some really
innovative things on Google

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:33.345
Cloud Platform with
security at scale.

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:40.780
OK so, on Google
Cloud Platform, we

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:45.000
want to make sure that
only specific users can

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.870
perform particular actions.

00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:49.137
I'm going to tell you the
first half of that story.

00:01:49.137 --> 00:01:51.470
I'm going to tell you about
identity management and user

00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:52.650
authentication.

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:56.640
And then I'll give you a
quick demo of it as well.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:59.510
So in the simplest
sense, you can

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:02.430
you just use Google accounts
to access the Google Cloud

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:04.150
Platform.

00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:06.030
But the issue there
is that you don't

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:09.410
have any centralized
management over these accounts.

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:13.390
So for organizations,
it's a much better option

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:16.490
to use a Google
for Work-- the user

00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:18.580
management-- to
create and manage

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:20.355
the accounts and credentials.

00:02:20.355 --> 00:02:22.730
Now note, I'm just talking
about the Google for Work user

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:24.340
management aspects.

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:28.100
You don't have to use the
applications like Gmail Docs

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:28.946
and Calendar.

00:02:28.946 --> 00:02:29.570
Though you can.

00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:30.528
They're pretty awesome.

00:02:30.528 --> 00:02:32.310
I recommend them.

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:34.080
So what this does
in particular is

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.930
it gives you a single
place to manage and control

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:37.950
your accounts.

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:40.340
And in particular, it
gives you an easy way

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:44.190
to suspend bad accounts quickly.

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:47.320
Because Google is
managing the login,

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:50.870
you get all the benefits and
security of the Google login

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:54.740
screen, and the password
reset mechanisms.

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:58.070
We have session and
device management tools

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:00.510
that help you know how
your users are accessing,

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:02.490
and where they're
logging in and when.

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:05.030
And we also detect
suspicious activity,

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:08.520
and we can configure
it to alert you.

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:11.030
OK, so this is a great
option for many companies.

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:13.770
It's the option I chose
as the CTO of a company

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:18.300
named Vendasta before
I joined Google.

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:21.210
Now on the other hand, many
of you probably already

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:23.450
have an identity system
that works great for you

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:25.130
and you're comfortable with.

00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:27.640
In this case, you can
enable single sign on

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:30.890
or SSO between Google
and that identity system.

00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:34.790
And you can continue using all
the practices and processes

00:03:34.790 --> 00:03:38.030
that you have in place today.

00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:41.550
With SSO configure, whenever
an unauthenticated user

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:44.360
is trying to access
the system, we'll

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:47.240
bounce the user over
to your ID system

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:51.630
to get credentials for them.

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:53.320
OK?

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:56.610
Now, the corresponding
user account

00:03:56.610 --> 00:03:57.970
needs to exist in Google.

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:00.440
So we have to sync up the
user names between your system

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:01.710
and our system.

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:03.630
And to help with
this, Google as a tool

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:06.910
called GADS, or Google
Apps Directory Sync.

00:04:06.910 --> 00:04:10.630
And what this tool does is it
syncs identities and groups

00:04:10.630 --> 00:04:13.762
from your LDAP over to Google.

00:04:13.762 --> 00:04:15.970
We'll talk a lot more about
groups in the second part

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:17.680
of this talk.

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:20.649
So Federated identity using
single sign on, using SSO,

00:04:20.649 --> 00:04:23.830
is used today by large
organizations like Coca Cola

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:25.800
and Smart Technologies.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:28.410
And I'll demo SSO for
you in a couple minutes.

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:35.450
To configure SSO, it's
really a simple process.

00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:37.610
All you need to
provide are three links

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:40.000
into your existing
authentication system--

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:42.400
one for sign in,
one for sign out,

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:44.469
and one for change password.

00:04:44.469 --> 00:04:46.510
And the change password
really could be anything,

00:04:46.510 --> 00:04:48.468
because it's not actually
part of the off-flow.

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:53.120
Our SSO is built
on SAML 2, which

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:57.991
is a secure industry standard
protocol for exchanging user

00:04:57.991 --> 00:04:58.490
assertions.

00:04:58.490 --> 00:05:01.430
And assertions are just
basically facts about the user

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:03.510
that Google will trust.

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:07.700
To prevent tampering,
SAML allows the assertions

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:10.550
to be signed--
digitally signed--

00:05:10.550 --> 00:05:14.180
so we can verify that the
information originated from you

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:18.010
and has not been tampered with.

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:22.440
If your identity system--
sorry, to allow that to work,

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:24.760
you have to provide one
more piece of configuration.

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:26.176
And that's the
digital certificate

00:05:26.176 --> 00:05:29.010
that we'll use to
validate the signature.

00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:32.090
If your identity system
today doesn't support SAML 2,

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:35.930
you can use third-party
identity management plug-ins,

00:05:35.930 --> 00:05:38.680
like Ping and Octa and
others to bridge that gap.

00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:47.920
So let's take a little bit of
a look at this SAML exchange.

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:49.240
There it is in all its glory.

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:50.830
Isn't it wonderful?

00:05:50.830 --> 00:05:52.835
Hope you can read
this at the back.

00:05:52.835 --> 00:05:54.710
I'd like to take some
time to go through each

00:05:54.710 --> 00:05:58.612
of these lines in detail and
describe what's happening.

00:05:58.612 --> 00:05:59.695
So let's start at the top.

00:06:02.590 --> 00:06:03.590
All right.

00:06:03.590 --> 00:06:04.670
I'm just kidding.

00:06:04.670 --> 00:06:06.142
I really do like this stuff.

00:06:06.142 --> 00:06:08.350
And if you want to talk
about it, come find me later.

00:06:08.350 --> 00:06:08.850
We can tall.

00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:10.830
But I'm not going to make
you sit through this.

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:13.890
I see some people kind of
turning for the doors, so--

00:06:13.890 --> 00:06:16.710
Suffice to say, what
we do is we redirect

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:19.950
the user to your identity
system with a SAML request.

00:06:19.950 --> 00:06:23.530
You authenticate the user
and redirect back to Google

00:06:23.530 --> 00:06:26.976
with a signed SAML response, in
particular containing the user

00:06:26.976 --> 00:06:28.170
name.

00:06:28.170 --> 00:06:31.250
That user name identifies
the appropriate account.

00:06:31.250 --> 00:06:34.070
On Google's side, we
create a user session,

00:06:34.070 --> 00:06:36.310
and we grant the user access.

00:06:36.310 --> 00:06:38.990
So in short, a whole
bunch of redirects.

00:06:38.990 --> 00:06:40.880
And this is
typically transparent

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:42.640
if a user's already signed in.

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:45.560
There's some XML
goop, and a signature,

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:47.380
and, boom, the user's
logged into Google.

00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:53.485
So I'd like to do
a demo of this.

00:06:53.485 --> 00:06:55.070
Can we just flip to
the demo, please?

00:06:57.908 --> 00:06:58.860
Great.

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:03.230
So a typical case would be
that a developer or DevOps

00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:06.660
person wants to access the
Cloud console to do something

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:11.070
like start a VM or check
logs or a justifier firewall.

00:07:11.070 --> 00:07:14.160
And so to do so, they point
the browser to the console.

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:21.220
So let me see if this
is going to work.

00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:23.380
OK.

00:07:23.380 --> 00:07:24.620
All right.

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:26.020
So they're headed
to the console.

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:29.350
Because the user is
not authenticated,

00:07:29.350 --> 00:07:32.690
we're going to some
redirects happen.

00:07:32.690 --> 00:07:34.750
OK, so first of all, we're
at this Google screen

00:07:34.750 --> 00:07:37.447
asking for a user name.

00:07:37.447 --> 00:07:38.030
You know what?

00:07:38.030 --> 00:07:39.780
There's no password
here, because we

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:41.880
don't know if we need to
have a password or not.

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:43.640
In single sign
on, Google doesn't

00:07:43.640 --> 00:07:47.500
have any the passwords or even
any of the password hashes.

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:49.680
So we have to ask
the user what domain

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:51.520
they want to authenticate to.

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:55.290
For example, if they enter
it @gmail.com domain,

00:07:55.290 --> 00:07:57.150
we know that Google
controls the credentials

00:07:57.150 --> 00:08:00.830
and has to be responsible
for gathering the login.

00:08:00.830 --> 00:08:07.250
However the enter domain
that's configured for SSO--

00:08:07.250 --> 00:08:13.200
I've set up weston-widgets.com
as an SSO-enabled domain.

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:15.690
Google will see that
and redirect the user

00:08:15.690 --> 00:08:19.150
to the configured sign in link.

00:08:19.150 --> 00:08:20.770
Great, it worked.

00:08:20.770 --> 00:08:25.460
So here we are at my design
challenge login screen

00:08:25.460 --> 00:08:27.080
for Weston Widgets.

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:29.530
And this is where you gather
the credentials for the user.

00:08:29.530 --> 00:08:31.780
And you might do that, in
this case, through password.

00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:36.130
Or maybe use facial recognition
or a chip implant or something

00:08:36.130 --> 00:08:37.510
crazy like that.

00:08:37.510 --> 00:08:38.510
jake@weston-widgets.com.

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:45.490
So when I authenticate,
this login system

00:08:45.490 --> 00:08:47.970
is going to generate a SAML
response containing the user

00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:48.470
name.

00:08:48.470 --> 00:08:50.840
It will sign it with a
digital six certificate,

00:08:50.840 --> 00:08:53.250
redirect the user
back to Google.

00:08:53.250 --> 00:08:55.180
Google is going to
validate the signature.

00:08:55.180 --> 00:08:57.010
It's going to look
at the user name.

00:08:57.010 --> 00:08:59.010
It's going to find the
corresponding account,

00:08:59.010 --> 00:09:00.879
create a user session,
and then redirect

00:09:00.879 --> 00:09:03.170
the user to the Cloud console
where they are ultimately

00:09:03.170 --> 00:09:04.240
headed.

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:07.596
So keep an eye on that URL
bar up-- the address bar--

00:09:07.596 --> 00:09:09.220
and see these redirects
in action here.

00:09:13.012 --> 00:09:14.440
All right.

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:18.030
We have made it to
the Cloud console.

00:09:18.030 --> 00:09:22.330
And you can-- and
you can see that I'm

00:09:22.330 --> 00:09:27.760
logged in as the user I intended
to be logged in as, Jake.

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:31.290
This same technique can work
anywhere you need an OAuth

00:09:31.290 --> 00:09:32.560
access token as well.

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:36.320
So when you go to give
an OAuth access token,

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:37.790
the user has to grant that.

00:09:37.790 --> 00:09:39.610
And so we need an
authenticated user.

00:09:39.610 --> 00:09:41.790
So you flip into the OAuth flow.

00:09:41.790 --> 00:09:44.520
And then, that will
flip into the Auth flow,

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:47.130
which will ultimately go through
the single sign in process.

00:09:47.130 --> 00:09:51.240
So this same technique
works for the GCloud command

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:54.090
line interface-- the Cloud SDK.

00:09:54.090 --> 00:09:59.170
It also works for any APIs
that use OAuth as well.

00:09:59.170 --> 00:10:01.884
You'll notice that there
is a sign out link here.

00:10:01.884 --> 00:10:03.300
And remember in
the configuration,

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:06.005
we had a sign out
configuration as well.

00:10:06.005 --> 00:10:07.380
And so when I
click this, this is

00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:10.020
going to sign me
out of both Google

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:12.620
and your on-premise system.

00:10:12.620 --> 00:10:17.150
So if I go ahead and click that,
I've logged out of both systems

00:10:17.150 --> 00:10:17.650
now.

00:10:17.650 --> 00:10:19.220
So I've achieved
single sign out.

00:10:22.510 --> 00:10:23.280
All right.

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:24.640
So one other quick demo here.

00:10:27.630 --> 00:10:30.170
Google App Engine is
an excellent platform

00:10:30.170 --> 00:10:33.020
as a service to easily
and quickly build

00:10:33.020 --> 00:10:34.990
a line of business applications.

00:10:34.990 --> 00:10:38.450
So for example, order
management, or customer support

00:10:38.450 --> 00:10:41.140
systems, human resource
information systems,

00:10:41.140 --> 00:10:42.470
things like that.

00:10:42.470 --> 00:10:48.590
But how can we secure those
apps with your identity system?

00:10:48.590 --> 00:10:51.130
With Google App Engine, this
is literally one line of code

00:10:51.130 --> 00:10:53.050
away.

00:10:53.050 --> 00:10:55.000
First of all, what
we want to do is

00:10:55.000 --> 00:10:57.010
configure the App
Engine application

00:10:57.010 --> 00:10:59.010
to be scoped to your domain.

00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:01.590
So in case of Weston
Widgets, I can set it

00:11:01.590 --> 00:11:05.050
up so that only Weston
Widgets users can have access

00:11:05.050 --> 00:11:07.370
to the application.

00:11:07.370 --> 00:11:12.400
Then in my app.YAML--
This is the wrong window.

00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:17.380
This is the right
window-- all I need to do

00:11:17.380 --> 00:11:18.429
is add one line of code.

00:11:18.429 --> 00:11:19.720
Here's the single line of code.

00:11:19.720 --> 00:11:21.386
That's actually a
line of configuration.

00:11:21.386 --> 00:11:23.450
It says, login required.

00:11:23.450 --> 00:11:25.000
I put that on my handler.

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:26.500
I save that and deploy that.

00:11:26.500 --> 00:11:29.890
So imagine this is the intranet
app for Weston Widgets.

00:11:34.530 --> 00:11:39.840
I will go to the intranet app.

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:42.790
And again, because
I'm not authenticated,

00:11:42.790 --> 00:11:45.832
the login required
tells Google App Engine

00:11:45.832 --> 00:11:48.290
that we need to kick the user
into the authentication flow.

00:11:52.820 --> 00:11:53.320
Whoa.

00:11:57.750 --> 00:11:59.872
That is not my app at all.

00:11:59.872 --> 00:12:00.580
I've been hacked.

00:12:03.130 --> 00:12:03.950
There we go.

00:12:03.950 --> 00:12:06.650
I don't know what
I typed before.

00:12:06.650 --> 00:12:08.524
So here I am at
the login screen.

00:12:08.524 --> 00:12:09.940
Hey, did you notice
that we didn't

00:12:09.940 --> 00:12:12.106
hit that Google screen that
asked for the user name,

00:12:12.106 --> 00:12:13.090
like we did last time?

00:12:13.090 --> 00:12:15.460
In this case, because the
App Engine application

00:12:15.460 --> 00:12:18.259
I scoped to Weston
Widgets, Google already

00:12:18.259 --> 00:12:20.800
knows where it has to send the
user to get the authentication

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:22.080
information.

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:25.417
So I can go ahead
and log in yet again.

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:35.680
And again, redirection
madness ensues.

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:38.640
And here I am at
my intranet app.

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:42.760
So you can see once again, I
am logged in as the right user.

00:12:42.760 --> 00:12:45.980
And the Google App
Engine user API

00:12:45.980 --> 00:12:50.570
makes this very easy to get
the user name from the user.

00:12:50.570 --> 00:12:52.320
So there you have it.

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:56.490
To get single sign on configured
with Google Cloud Platform, all

00:12:56.490 --> 00:12:59.160
you need is three links
into your existing identity

00:12:59.160 --> 00:13:00.850
system and a
digital certificate,

00:13:00.850 --> 00:13:03.370
and you're off to the races.

00:13:03.370 --> 00:13:05.280
Piece of cake.

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:06.730
So now I'm going
to throw it over

00:13:06.730 --> 00:13:09.960
to Rae for the much
cooler part of the talk.

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:12.760
Once we know who
the user is, how

00:13:12.760 --> 00:13:14.910
we control what
they have access to?

00:13:14.910 --> 00:13:17.897
And can you just flip
back to the slides please?

00:13:17.897 --> 00:13:18.835
There you go Rae.

00:13:22.120 --> 00:13:22.740
RAE WANG: OK.

00:13:22.740 --> 00:13:24.080
Thank you Jason.

00:13:24.080 --> 00:13:27.335
So now, your user has been
authenticated in the system.

00:13:27.335 --> 00:13:29.710
The next thing we want to make
sure we can get them to do

00:13:29.710 --> 00:13:34.790
is to get authorized to access
to right set of resources.

00:13:34.790 --> 00:13:38.680
So let's talk about the AM
part of Iam access management,

00:13:38.680 --> 00:13:43.170
meaning who can do what
and which resources?

00:13:43.170 --> 00:13:46.760
The can do what part is
defined by an Iam role.

00:13:46.760 --> 00:13:48.820
Now, when you think about
it, most of the time,

00:13:48.820 --> 00:13:51.480
when you're trying to do
any meaningful operation,

00:13:51.480 --> 00:13:54.250
it takes more than just
a single permission.

00:13:54.250 --> 00:13:56.850
In this example, if you're
trying to get somebody

00:13:56.850 --> 00:14:00.640
to be able to manage the
VM instances in a project,

00:14:00.640 --> 00:14:04.530
they need to computer instances
to get, and create and delete

00:14:04.530 --> 00:14:06.280
and start and stop and so on.

00:14:06.280 --> 00:14:09.040
So we grouped these
permissions into an Iam role

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:12.310
to simplify your
management experience.

00:14:12.310 --> 00:14:14.710
To grant the right
permissions to user,

00:14:14.710 --> 00:14:19.640
you grant a user one or
more Iam roles resource.

00:14:19.640 --> 00:14:23.910
So in this example here,
we're giving a group of users

00:14:23.910 --> 00:14:27.510
the instance admin
role on Project A which

00:14:27.510 --> 00:14:30.300
allows them to then go
and manage the GCEBM

00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:31.625
instances in a project.

00:14:35.120 --> 00:14:37.640
Now this is what
the API looks like.

00:14:37.640 --> 00:14:41.580
To set an Iam policy, you
call the setIam policy method

00:14:41.580 --> 00:14:44.220
and the resource where
you're setting the policy.

00:14:44.220 --> 00:14:46.380
Since you already know
what the resource is,

00:14:46.380 --> 00:14:50.020
the body of the policy contains
the other two parts in your Iam

00:14:50.020 --> 00:14:53.290
equation-- the roles,
and then which members

00:14:53.290 --> 00:14:55.480
you're granting each role to.

00:14:55.480 --> 00:14:57.550
So as you can see in
this example here,

00:14:57.550 --> 00:15:00.250
a member can be a number
of different things.

00:15:00.250 --> 00:15:03.460
It can be a user, a
group, a service account,

00:15:03.460 --> 00:15:05.290
which we're going
to talk about later,

00:15:05.290 --> 00:15:07.873
as well as, if you want to share
with everybody in the domain,

00:15:07.873 --> 00:15:09.550
you can just put a
domain name there.

00:15:09.550 --> 00:15:11.425
And if you want to share
with the world-- say

00:15:11.425 --> 00:15:13.120
you have a very public
storage bucket--

00:15:13.120 --> 00:15:16.540
you can specify all
authenticated users.

00:15:16.540 --> 00:15:19.450
You use the set method to
set the policy on resource,

00:15:19.450 --> 00:15:20.820
get it back with the get method.

00:15:20.820 --> 00:15:22.210
Standard stuff.

00:15:22.210 --> 00:15:25.190
And then there's this method
called testIamPermissions.

00:15:25.190 --> 00:15:27.270
And this is used
for you to check

00:15:27.270 --> 00:15:29.830
if a user has the
permission to execute

00:15:29.830 --> 00:15:31.850
a very specific operation.

00:15:31.850 --> 00:15:34.310
And it comes very handy if
you're building your own custom

00:15:34.310 --> 00:15:35.752
tool, your own
custom UI, and you

00:15:35.752 --> 00:15:39.250
can decide whether to show
or hide a button for a user,

00:15:39.250 --> 00:15:41.810
depending on what
permissions they have.

00:15:41.810 --> 00:15:44.960
So these methods on a project
level have already GA'd.

00:15:44.960 --> 00:15:47.800
So now you can use the API
to programmatically manage

00:15:47.800 --> 00:15:48.800
the project permissions.

00:15:51.560 --> 00:15:53.620
Now let's talk a little
bit about the roles.

00:15:53.620 --> 00:15:56.435
If you're a current
GCP user, you probably

00:15:56.435 --> 00:15:58.570
already have used one
of the three roles

00:15:58.570 --> 00:16:02.040
on the project level--
owner, editor, and viewer.

00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:04.659
Many of you have told
us that they're not

00:16:04.659 --> 00:16:06.450
granular enough for
you to really lock down

00:16:06.450 --> 00:16:08.310
your permissions, right?

00:16:08.310 --> 00:16:10.660
So we know three is
obviously to few.

00:16:10.660 --> 00:16:12.752
Let's try 23.

00:16:12.752 --> 00:16:14.460
We're happy to announce
that we have just

00:16:14.460 --> 00:16:17.490
launched over 20 new Iam
roles across of a set

00:16:17.490 --> 00:16:19.950
of major services.

00:16:19.950 --> 00:16:22.450
Let's look at some examples.

00:16:22.450 --> 00:16:24.370
You often want to
allow your developers

00:16:24.370 --> 00:16:27.310
to be able to access logs
so they can do debugging.

00:16:27.310 --> 00:16:30.110
But you might want to expose the
rest of the production projects

00:16:30.110 --> 00:16:30.980
to them.

00:16:30.980 --> 00:16:33.880
And you can do so by granting
them the log viewer role,

00:16:33.880 --> 00:16:36.470
so they can only
look at the logs.

00:16:36.470 --> 00:16:39.490
If you have a CI or
CD system, and you

00:16:39.490 --> 00:16:42.120
want to allow them to
automatically update

00:16:42.120 --> 00:16:45.000
bits on GA and GCE,
you can grant them

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:48.310
and the [INAUDIBLE] deployer
role or the compute instance

00:16:48.310 --> 00:16:49.200
Iaming role.

00:16:49.200 --> 00:16:52.010
And that way, they can make
the update on the instances

00:16:52.010 --> 00:16:55.440
without being able to
access data and storage.

00:16:55.440 --> 00:16:57.260
You can use one of
the data flow roles

00:16:57.260 --> 00:16:59.150
to give your data
scientists access

00:16:59.150 --> 00:17:02.760
to interact with data flow
jobs without allowing them

00:17:02.760 --> 00:17:05.760
to accidentally deleting the
VMs running underneath the data

00:17:05.760 --> 00:17:07.589
flow.

00:17:07.589 --> 00:17:11.810
And the common use case for GCS
is to allow a user or service

00:17:11.810 --> 00:17:13.847
to upload data.

00:17:13.847 --> 00:17:15.930
And you can lock down the
permissions in that case

00:17:15.930 --> 00:17:18.990
by granting them the storage
object creator role, which

00:17:18.990 --> 00:17:21.060
allows them to
create new objects,

00:17:21.060 --> 00:17:23.930
but not have permission to
access or delete the existing

00:17:23.930 --> 00:17:27.720
data in the storage bucket.

00:17:27.720 --> 00:17:28.984
And you might say, wait, wait.

00:17:28.984 --> 00:17:30.900
But there are a couple
of roles I really need,

00:17:30.900 --> 00:17:32.050
and they're not on that list.

00:17:32.050 --> 00:17:33.674
What are you going
to do for me, right?

00:17:33.674 --> 00:17:35.540
I see a lot of nodding heads.

00:17:35.540 --> 00:17:37.880
Well, our teams are
working very, very hard

00:17:37.880 --> 00:17:40.160
on the next wave of
roles, as well as

00:17:40.160 --> 00:17:42.746
giving you the ability to
eventually make your own custom

00:17:42.746 --> 00:17:43.245
roles.

00:17:45.960 --> 00:17:49.300
Now can we flip over
to the demo screen?

00:17:49.300 --> 00:17:52.080
I'm going to show you how
these roles work for real.

00:17:52.080 --> 00:17:55.010
So here I have a project where
I have the traditional owner

00:17:55.010 --> 00:17:58.620
role, which means I can do just
about anything in the project.

00:17:58.620 --> 00:18:02.900
I can look at compute and
storage and my API usage,

00:18:02.900 --> 00:18:05.070
as well as billing data.

00:18:05.070 --> 00:18:06.970
What I can also
do on a project is

00:18:06.970 --> 00:18:09.430
to go over to the
Permissions tab

00:18:09.430 --> 00:18:14.430
where I can see how many roles
I have assigned to which users.

00:18:14.430 --> 00:18:16.940
As you can see, I have
a number of human users

00:18:16.940 --> 00:18:19.487
who have the owner role.

00:18:19.487 --> 00:18:21.320
And then I have a number
of service accounts

00:18:21.320 --> 00:18:24.310
who have the editor role.

00:18:24.310 --> 00:18:27.520
And to assign additional roles
to people, all I need to do

00:18:27.520 --> 00:18:29.990
is to click this Add
Members button, which

00:18:29.990 --> 00:18:31.400
brings up a dialogue.

00:18:31.400 --> 00:18:35.980
I can type a user's
email address

00:18:35.980 --> 00:18:38.550
and then select one of the
roles to assign to them.

00:18:41.290 --> 00:18:44.180
Now let's come to the second
project, where I don't have

00:18:44.180 --> 00:18:45.689
the owner, editor, viewer role.

00:18:45.689 --> 00:18:48.230
And therefore, I shouldn't be
able to see most of information

00:18:48.230 --> 00:18:48.730
here.

00:18:48.730 --> 00:18:51.240
So the dashboard
is hidden from me

00:18:51.240 --> 00:18:53.330
because I can't see the data.

00:18:53.330 --> 00:18:56.510
I also can't access App
Engine or storage or billing.

00:18:56.510 --> 00:18:59.900
However, I do have one role
on this project which is

00:18:59.900 --> 00:19:02.030
to compute instant adding role.

00:19:02.030 --> 00:19:04.830
So what this role allows me to
do is come to the compute page

00:19:04.830 --> 00:19:08.680
and be able to see a list of
my VMs and control the VMs.

00:19:08.680 --> 00:19:11.500
You can see the buttons, like
creating instance and instance

00:19:11.500 --> 00:19:14.340
group, are available to
me because of that method

00:19:14.340 --> 00:19:16.520
we talked about, test Iam
permission, checks that I

00:19:16.520 --> 00:19:19.370
have permission to
execute these operations.

00:19:19.370 --> 00:19:22.880
Can we go back to the slides?

00:19:22.880 --> 00:19:25.330
Thank you.

00:19:25.330 --> 00:19:26.130
All right.

00:19:26.130 --> 00:19:29.130
Since Iam policies
are set on resources,

00:19:29.130 --> 00:19:32.300
let's talk a little more about
how resources are defined.

00:19:32.300 --> 00:19:34.820
Resources are not just
a random flat list

00:19:34.820 --> 00:19:37.290
of VMs and GCS buckets.

00:19:37.290 --> 00:19:39.700
They have relationships
with each other.

00:19:39.700 --> 00:19:42.120
And we can use these
relationships to simplify

00:19:42.120 --> 00:19:44.660
how you can manage policies.

00:19:44.660 --> 00:19:46.700
If you're a current
user, you've already

00:19:46.700 --> 00:19:49.470
used this concept
called a project.

00:19:49.470 --> 00:19:54.390
A project is a basic container
of resources, and a trust unit.

00:19:54.390 --> 00:19:56.870
The roles that we have just
seen the slides in the demo

00:19:56.870 --> 00:19:59.050
can be assigned on
the project level.

00:19:59.050 --> 00:20:02.660
And when you do that, they get
inherited by all the resources

00:20:02.660 --> 00:20:04.820
underneath the project.

00:20:04.820 --> 00:20:06.670
So for example,
what we just saw,

00:20:06.670 --> 00:20:10.950
you assigned the instance
admin role to a set of users.

00:20:10.950 --> 00:20:14.100
Then these users can
manage all the instances

00:20:14.100 --> 00:20:15.691
within the project.

00:20:15.691 --> 00:20:17.940
You don't have to go and
permission each individual VM

00:20:17.940 --> 00:20:18.810
instance.

00:20:18.810 --> 00:20:20.470
And when your out
of [? scaler ?]

00:20:20.470 --> 00:20:22.420
spins up another
new instance, you

00:20:22.420 --> 00:20:24.970
don't have to go and hurry up
and add a policy for your admin

00:20:24.970 --> 00:20:26.390
to do their job.

00:20:26.390 --> 00:20:29.240
They get us through inheritance.

00:20:29.240 --> 00:20:31.580
Now some of the resources
underneath a project

00:20:31.580 --> 00:20:34.980
can also be permissioned
individually-- for example,

00:20:34.980 --> 00:20:37.830
pops up topics or GCS buckets.

00:20:37.830 --> 00:20:39.420
And I know a lot
of you want to be

00:20:39.420 --> 00:20:41.590
able to do that on other
types of resources,

00:20:41.590 --> 00:20:44.500
especially VMs come
up a lot, right?

00:20:44.500 --> 00:20:46.452
Our teams are working
hard to find a solution

00:20:46.452 --> 00:20:47.910
to allow you to be
able to do that.

00:20:50.750 --> 00:20:52.985
Some of you have been a
GCP user a for a while.

00:20:52.985 --> 00:20:55.280
And you know as the
usage grows, you

00:20:55.280 --> 00:20:58.300
might end up with a
large number of projects.

00:20:58.300 --> 00:21:01.730
And then it can get a little
unwieldy to manage at scale.

00:21:01.730 --> 00:21:04.075
And that's why we have
introduced now the organization

00:21:04.075 --> 00:21:05.560
node.

00:21:05.560 --> 00:21:09.550
The organization node is a layer
that sits above your projects.

00:21:09.550 --> 00:21:14.330
It's a company's root node for
managing your GCP resources.

00:21:14.330 --> 00:21:17.640
If you're a Google for Work
customer, when you enable

00:21:17.640 --> 00:21:21.440
the organization node, then
from there on, whenever

00:21:21.440 --> 00:21:24.550
any identity from your domain
goes and creates a new project,

00:21:24.550 --> 00:21:26.940
whether it's a service account
or user, when you create

00:21:26.940 --> 00:21:29.340
a project, the project
will automatically

00:21:29.340 --> 00:21:31.200
belong to your
organization node.

00:21:31.200 --> 00:21:33.850
So no more shadow
projects and rogue admins.

00:21:33.850 --> 00:21:35.986
You have control of at all.

00:21:35.986 --> 00:21:38.360
And there are a couple of
roles that we have specifically

00:21:38.360 --> 00:21:40.300
designed for the
organization node.

00:21:40.300 --> 00:21:42.680
One is the organization admin.

00:21:42.680 --> 00:21:45.270
Just like what the name
says, it allows your admin

00:21:45.270 --> 00:21:47.780
to have central
visibility and control

00:21:47.780 --> 00:21:50.090
of all of the projects,
all of the resources

00:21:50.090 --> 00:21:52.300
inside the company.

00:21:52.300 --> 00:21:54.720
Another interesting role is
the project creator role.

00:21:54.720 --> 00:21:57.390
So if you want to actually
control who in your company

00:21:57.390 --> 00:22:00.450
can set up new projects
to create new resources,

00:22:00.450 --> 00:22:04.200
you can use that role to
define that set of users.

00:22:04.200 --> 00:22:07.350
Also, other roles that you saw
when we looked at the project

00:22:07.350 --> 00:22:10.930
demo, they can also be granted
at the organization level.

00:22:10.930 --> 00:22:13.490
And again, because of
the policy inheritance,

00:22:13.490 --> 00:22:15.790
when they're granted
for the organization,

00:22:15.790 --> 00:22:19.020
they get inherited by all the
projects and all the resources

00:22:19.020 --> 00:22:21.410
inside the organization.

00:22:21.410 --> 00:22:23.357
So if you have a
networking team,

00:22:23.357 --> 00:22:24.940
you want to allow
your networking team

00:22:24.940 --> 00:22:27.190
to manage all the
networks in your company,

00:22:27.190 --> 00:22:29.080
you can grant them
the network admin role

00:22:29.080 --> 00:22:30.980
on the organization
level, which will

00:22:30.980 --> 00:22:33.760
allow them to manage
all the other networks,

00:22:33.760 --> 00:22:37.040
and all of the projects
inside your company.

00:22:37.040 --> 00:22:39.040
The organization node
is in alpha right now.

00:22:39.040 --> 00:22:42.000
So if you're our current
Google for Work customer,

00:22:42.000 --> 00:22:45.100
you can work with your account
manager or a sales partner

00:22:45.100 --> 00:22:49.260
to have one enabled
for your company.

00:22:49.260 --> 00:22:51.470
So let's take a look at a demo.

00:22:51.470 --> 00:22:52.880
Switch over?

00:22:52.880 --> 00:22:55.140
Thank you.

00:22:55.140 --> 00:22:59.420
So here I have a company,
which I conveniently

00:22:59.420 --> 00:23:04.236
named the Next Demo Company,
which has an organization node.

00:23:04.236 --> 00:23:05.860
And once you have an
organization node,

00:23:05.860 --> 00:23:07.670
you get an extra
drop down list, which

00:23:07.670 --> 00:23:09.560
is called the
Organization picker,

00:23:09.560 --> 00:23:12.780
where you can select to look at
things from your organization

00:23:12.780 --> 00:23:14.294
perspective.

00:23:14.294 --> 00:23:16.710
And when you do that, there
are two tabs available to you.

00:23:16.710 --> 00:23:18.360
One is the Projects
tab, where you

00:23:18.360 --> 00:23:21.780
get to see the entire list of
projects inside the company,

00:23:21.780 --> 00:23:23.940
regardless of whether
you personally

00:23:23.940 --> 00:23:27.190
have specific permission
that's printed on a project.

00:23:27.190 --> 00:23:29.680
You get that
through inheritance.

00:23:29.680 --> 00:23:31.890
And the second tab is
the Permissions tab,

00:23:31.890 --> 00:23:34.490
where you can view and
manage permissions granted

00:23:34.490 --> 00:23:36.500
on the organization level.

00:23:36.500 --> 00:23:39.272
And here, you can see
many of the Iam roles

00:23:39.272 --> 00:23:40.730
that we talked
about on the project

00:23:40.730 --> 00:23:44.550
level can also be granted
on the organization level.

00:23:44.550 --> 00:23:46.730
And the same dialogue
add members, again,

00:23:46.730 --> 00:23:50.080
will allow you to grant
additional roles to people

00:23:50.080 --> 00:23:51.850
on the org level.

00:23:51.850 --> 00:23:53.470
So if we look under
Project Creator,

00:23:53.470 --> 00:23:57.350
you can see that I've granted
this role to my entire domain,

00:23:57.350 --> 00:23:59.610
which means everybody
in my company

00:23:59.610 --> 00:24:02.587
can go and create new projects.

00:24:02.587 --> 00:24:03.670
Switch back to the slides.

00:24:07.740 --> 00:24:09.590
All right, so when we
previously told you

00:24:09.590 --> 00:24:13.270
that we've already told you
everything about identities,

00:24:13.270 --> 00:24:14.880
that wasn't completely right.

00:24:14.880 --> 00:24:16.410
Sorry, Jason.

00:24:16.410 --> 00:24:18.567
There's still one
type of identity

00:24:18.567 --> 00:24:20.150
that I want to talk
to you about here,

00:24:20.150 --> 00:24:22.760
which is a service account.

00:24:22.760 --> 00:24:26.050
Now a service account is not an
identity for your human beings,

00:24:26.050 --> 00:24:27.810
like user groups.

00:24:27.810 --> 00:24:30.250
But rather it's
the identity that

00:24:30.250 --> 00:24:32.920
can be used by your
programs to authenticate

00:24:32.920 --> 00:24:35.940
to access GCP APIs.

00:24:35.940 --> 00:24:38.650
Your users can go to web
page like Jason just did,

00:24:38.650 --> 00:24:40.750
and type a username
password and login.

00:24:40.750 --> 00:24:43.090
Well, your program
isn't going to do that.

00:24:43.090 --> 00:24:46.760
So instead, they
authenticate with keys.

00:24:46.760 --> 00:24:50.840
One or more keys can be created
for each Iam service account.

00:24:50.840 --> 00:24:54.290
And since keys basically give
you access to your resources,

00:24:54.290 --> 00:24:55.550
they're very sensitive data.

00:24:55.550 --> 00:24:58.740
You want to take
good care of them.

00:24:58.740 --> 00:25:02.309
If your programs run on App
Engine or Compute Engine,

00:25:02.309 --> 00:25:04.600
then you don't have to worry
about it because we manage

00:25:04.600 --> 00:25:06.330
the keys for you
and we regularly

00:25:06.330 --> 00:25:09.730
rotate the keys to
guard against leaking.

00:25:09.730 --> 00:25:11.620
If you run a program
somewhere else,

00:25:11.620 --> 00:25:15.280
you can download the keys
to be used in your own code.

00:25:15.280 --> 00:25:18.290
But keep them safe.

00:25:18.290 --> 00:25:20.730
But the curious thing
about service accounts

00:25:20.730 --> 00:25:25.120
is that a service account can be
both an identity and a resource

00:25:25.120 --> 00:25:27.040
in your Iam policy.

00:25:27.040 --> 00:25:28.770
And this is why.

00:25:28.770 --> 00:25:31.070
If you're using service
accounts for your VMs

00:25:31.070 --> 00:25:34.240
to route to access
GCP APIs, your VMs

00:25:34.240 --> 00:25:37.050
need permissions to
go to these APIs.

00:25:37.050 --> 00:25:39.851
And that permission are granted
through the service account.

00:25:39.851 --> 00:25:40.350
Right?

00:25:40.350 --> 00:25:43.350
In that case, you're granting
Iam roles on certain resources

00:25:43.350 --> 00:25:44.590
to the service account.

00:25:44.590 --> 00:25:47.650
And the service account
is acting as an identity,

00:25:47.650 --> 00:25:50.070
the identity of the VMs.

00:25:50.070 --> 00:25:51.800
However, you also
want to make sure

00:25:51.800 --> 00:25:54.340
that you can actually control
who has access to the service

00:25:54.340 --> 00:25:55.190
accounts.

00:25:55.190 --> 00:25:57.690
You don't want any random person
to be able to just bring up

00:25:57.690 --> 00:26:00.460
a VM and use a service
account and then

00:26:00.460 --> 00:26:02.510
they get access to
all your resources,

00:26:02.510 --> 00:26:03.500
and all bets are off.

00:26:03.500 --> 00:26:05.140
And that's not good.

00:26:05.140 --> 00:26:07.750
So, you can actually
commission the service account.

00:26:07.750 --> 00:26:10.780
For example, grant one or
more of your specific users

00:26:10.780 --> 00:26:13.070
we need to be able to do
things with these VMs.

00:26:13.070 --> 00:26:15.550
The service account
actor role to allow

00:26:15.550 --> 00:26:19.000
them to assume the identity
of the service account.

00:26:19.000 --> 00:26:20.750
Now in this case, in
this specific policy,

00:26:20.750 --> 00:26:22.750
the service account
is the resource

00:26:22.750 --> 00:26:23.916
that you want to permission.

00:26:26.520 --> 00:26:29.060
Another feature that we
have launched recently

00:26:29.060 --> 00:26:31.105
is per VM service account.

00:26:31.105 --> 00:26:34.030
Now, why do you need that?

00:26:34.030 --> 00:26:36.820
You need that so that your
different VMs and your project

00:26:36.820 --> 00:26:38.620
can run with different identity.

00:26:38.620 --> 00:26:41.710
And that gets different
sets of privileges.

00:26:41.710 --> 00:26:43.540
So in this example
here, in your project A,

00:26:43.540 --> 00:26:44.950
you have two components.

00:26:44.950 --> 00:26:47.050
Component A needs to
manage and therefore

00:26:47.050 --> 00:26:50.310
be the editor on Project
B, whereas component 2 just

00:26:50.310 --> 00:26:52.680
needs to be a storage bucket.

00:26:52.680 --> 00:26:56.050
You don't want to grant
your entire project's VMs

00:26:56.050 --> 00:26:58.400
the superset of all
of these permissions.

00:26:58.400 --> 00:27:01.450
Because that goes against
our security best practice.

00:27:01.450 --> 00:27:05.200
So instead what you do, is
you have two different service

00:27:05.200 --> 00:27:08.350
accounts representing these
two different components-- set

00:27:08.350 --> 00:27:10.950
of VMs And grant each
service account just

00:27:10.950 --> 00:27:14.040
a minimal set of privileges
that your VMs need

00:27:14.040 --> 00:27:16.750
to use to operate.

00:27:16.750 --> 00:27:19.490
You can also dynamically
change the privileges

00:27:19.490 --> 00:27:22.665
granted to the service
accounts while the VMs are

00:27:22.665 --> 00:27:27.770
running without having
to recreate our VMs

00:27:27.770 --> 00:27:29.440
All right, so now
you know what you can

00:27:29.440 --> 00:27:31.510
do with the new Iam features.

00:27:31.510 --> 00:27:33.677
Let's go through some
security best practices

00:27:33.677 --> 00:27:35.385
to talk about how to
secure environments.

00:27:38.020 --> 00:27:41.360
First of all, principle
of least privilege.

00:27:41.360 --> 00:27:45.260
This applies to all three
parts of your Iam policy.

00:27:45.260 --> 00:27:49.100
The identities, the roles,
as well as the resources.

00:27:49.100 --> 00:27:52.250
What not to do is to grant
everybody the owner role

00:27:52.250 --> 00:27:54.640
on your entire organization.

00:27:54.640 --> 00:27:55.772
I know we are good friends.

00:27:55.772 --> 00:27:56.480
We want to share.

00:27:56.480 --> 00:28:00.090
But this is risky, because
then any accidental mistake

00:28:00.090 --> 00:28:02.740
or an intentional hack can
just bring down your app

00:28:02.740 --> 00:28:04.340
or leak your data.

00:28:04.340 --> 00:28:07.640
So instead, you want to be very
specific and very deliberate

00:28:07.640 --> 00:28:08.720
in your policies.

00:28:08.720 --> 00:28:12.370
For example, granting
your security admin group

00:28:12.370 --> 00:28:16.020
A, the security admin role,
which allows them to change

00:28:16.020 --> 00:28:19.310
SSL certs and firewall
rules on specific projects

00:28:19.310 --> 00:28:23.010
that they need to work on.

00:28:23.010 --> 00:28:24.960
Use groups.

00:28:24.960 --> 00:28:27.050
Managing permissions
on individual users

00:28:27.050 --> 00:28:30.400
can quickly get both
cumbersome and error-prone.

00:28:30.400 --> 00:28:32.370
So you want to use groups.

00:28:32.370 --> 00:28:34.631
Let's say you have
a SecOps team.

00:28:34.631 --> 00:28:36.130
You want to create
a group for them.

00:28:36.130 --> 00:28:39.590
So as new members come into
a company and join the team,

00:28:39.590 --> 00:28:41.880
all you need to do is to
add them to the group,

00:28:41.880 --> 00:28:44.210
and all of a sudden, they
have the same privileges

00:28:44.210 --> 00:28:46.409
as the rest of their peers.

00:28:46.409 --> 00:28:48.700
And you might want to assign
more than just single role

00:28:48.700 --> 00:28:49.241
to the group.

00:28:49.241 --> 00:28:51.010
It depends on the job function.

00:28:51.010 --> 00:28:53.110
For example, here
your security team

00:28:53.110 --> 00:28:56.070
might need a security admin
role to do the firewall SSL

00:28:56.070 --> 00:28:58.170
[? source ?] change,
and allow the ORO just

00:28:58.170 --> 00:29:00.550
to monitor what's going on.

00:29:00.550 --> 00:29:03.170
And then as time goes on, their
job function might change,

00:29:03.170 --> 00:29:05.582
and the set of Iam
roles might change.

00:29:05.582 --> 00:29:07.290
And all you have to
do to stay up to date

00:29:07.290 --> 00:29:11.397
is to change the set of
Iam roles granted to group.

00:29:11.397 --> 00:29:12.980
Again, you won't
have to go and modify

00:29:12.980 --> 00:29:14.640
that for each individual
users, and try

00:29:14.640 --> 00:29:17.930
to keep them consistent.

00:29:17.930 --> 00:29:20.390
The Iam policies
allow you to secure

00:29:20.390 --> 00:29:22.400
who can access your resources.

00:29:22.400 --> 00:29:24.210
But you also want
to safeguard who

00:29:24.210 --> 00:29:28.020
can change these policies, which
is also granted through an Iam

00:29:28.020 --> 00:29:32.830
permission, as well as who can
change user group membership.

00:29:32.830 --> 00:29:35.470
Because either of those two
can allow additional people

00:29:35.470 --> 00:29:38.880
to access your resources.

00:29:38.880 --> 00:29:41.620
Another way to
safeguard your policies

00:29:41.620 --> 00:29:43.147
is through audit logging.

00:29:43.147 --> 00:29:45.730
Some of you might have gone to
the audit log session yesterday

00:29:45.730 --> 00:29:48.025
and have heard that
we now have support

00:29:48.025 --> 00:29:50.647
for audit log for project
level permission changes.

00:29:50.647 --> 00:29:52.730
And we're going to be
adding it for other resource

00:29:52.730 --> 00:29:55.742
levels in the next few months.

00:29:55.742 --> 00:29:57.200
And if you use
service accounts, we

00:29:57.200 --> 00:29:59.690
talk about how keys are
really sensitive data.

00:29:59.690 --> 00:30:01.240
And you can guard
against leaking

00:30:01.240 --> 00:30:03.600
by regularly rotating them.

00:30:03.600 --> 00:30:06.700
And you can do so by
creating a new key

00:30:06.700 --> 00:30:09.420
and updating a program
to use the new key

00:30:09.420 --> 00:30:11.830
and then go remove the old key.

00:30:11.830 --> 00:30:14.420
And all of these APIs, including
the API [INAUDIBLE] list

00:30:14.420 --> 00:30:16.350
to create and manage
your service accounts

00:30:16.350 --> 00:30:20.930
are part of the Iam API,
which is now in beta.

00:30:20.930 --> 00:30:23.740
So we promised you this target's
going to keep getting better.

00:30:23.740 --> 00:30:26.330
Now comes to the
most exciting part.

00:30:26.330 --> 00:30:29.300
You're going to hear from a
couple of real GCP customers

00:30:29.300 --> 00:30:33.170
on how they have used
the Iam features.

00:30:33.170 --> 00:30:35.220
First we're going
to have SnapChat.

00:30:35.220 --> 00:30:37.810
And as you know,
SnapChat rounds on GCP

00:30:37.810 --> 00:30:40.750
and serves over
100 million users.

00:30:40.750 --> 00:30:43.061
And without a private
user data SnapChat has,

00:30:43.061 --> 00:30:44.560
it's no wonder that
they really care

00:30:44.560 --> 00:30:46.270
about security and privacy.

00:30:46.270 --> 00:30:49.130
So we have Subhash from
the SnapChat security team.

00:30:56.300 --> 00:30:59.050
SUBHASH SANKURATRIPATI:
Thanks, Rae.

00:30:59.050 --> 00:31:02.300
So for those of you that
don't know what SnapChat is,

00:31:02.300 --> 00:31:04.710
we're a storytelling
social network.

00:31:04.710 --> 00:31:11.280
We have a large number of users
that log in on a daily basis.

00:31:11.280 --> 00:31:13.140
The current user
numbers are somewhere

00:31:13.140 --> 00:31:16.820
upwards of 100 million-plus
daily users that actually

00:31:16.820 --> 00:31:21.180
log in or interact with our
servers using our mobile apps,

00:31:21.180 --> 00:31:23.940
viewing about eight-plus
billion videos.

00:31:23.940 --> 00:31:27.230
And thanks in large part to the
Google Cloud and App Engine,

00:31:27.230 --> 00:31:30.240
we're able to service that
large number of requests

00:31:30.240 --> 00:31:32.510
with a very small
number of engineers.

00:31:32.510 --> 00:31:35.340
We're about a 200-plus
engineering size team.

00:31:35.340 --> 00:31:39.680
And the other part that
as a security engineer,

00:31:39.680 --> 00:31:42.390
the other advantage that
the Cloud gives us is we're

00:31:42.390 --> 00:31:44.290
largely responsible
for making sure

00:31:44.290 --> 00:31:47.420
that the security of a project
or a cross-project interactions

00:31:47.420 --> 00:31:48.055
are secure.

00:31:48.055 --> 00:31:49.430
But we don't have
to really worry

00:31:49.430 --> 00:31:52.280
about how the underlying
infrastructure is built.

00:31:52.280 --> 00:31:54.520
That is Google's responsibility.

00:31:54.520 --> 00:31:58.230
Effectively, what we
are responsible for

00:31:58.230 --> 00:32:01.055
is understanding where the
Google security contract ends,

00:32:01.055 --> 00:32:02.870
and pick it up from there.

00:32:02.870 --> 00:32:05.660
And prior to the advent
of the organization node,

00:32:05.660 --> 00:32:08.920
we had a very strict policy
on creating new projects.

00:32:08.920 --> 00:32:11.800
Because A, anybody
could create a project,

00:32:11.800 --> 00:32:15.290
and we wouldn't even know
that such a project existed.

00:32:15.290 --> 00:32:17.540
So with the advent of
the organization node,

00:32:17.540 --> 00:32:18.540
I think it will help us.

00:32:18.540 --> 00:32:21.400
We'll probably have
a lot more projects.

00:32:21.400 --> 00:32:25.090
And next, I'm going to
introduce a customer that

00:32:25.090 --> 00:32:29.580
apparently has a
lot larger footprint

00:32:29.580 --> 00:32:30.970
that what SnapChat does.

00:32:30.970 --> 00:32:34.807
I'm going to introduce David
from the Google SecOps team.

00:32:38.623 --> 00:32:40.540
DAVID "CACHE" MCWHERTER: Hi.

00:32:40.540 --> 00:32:45.600
My name is David, and I work on
the Google's security ops team.

00:32:45.600 --> 00:32:47.590
And basically, my
job is to make sure

00:32:47.590 --> 00:32:52.912
that all of Google's usage of
GCP is secure and protected.

00:32:52.912 --> 00:32:54.370
What most of you
might not know, is

00:32:54.370 --> 00:32:56.550
that Google has
been for a while now

00:32:56.550 --> 00:32:59.590
a fairly heavy
user of GCP itself.

00:32:59.590 --> 00:33:03.490
We have over 100,000
GCP projects running out

00:33:03.490 --> 00:33:06.120
in the wild already running
all sorts of interesting apps

00:33:06.120 --> 00:33:08.890
from our HR tooling
to compensation

00:33:08.890 --> 00:33:12.100
to internal incident
response management tools.

00:33:12.100 --> 00:33:14.922
And Subhash, we even
have a social network.

00:33:14.922 --> 00:33:16.230
I'm just saying.

00:33:16.230 --> 00:33:16.730
Anyway.

00:33:20.020 --> 00:33:21.440
There we go.

00:33:21.440 --> 00:33:24.100
So Subhash and I, we're going
to talk to you a little bit

00:33:24.100 --> 00:33:27.050
about how do we-- two things.

00:33:27.050 --> 00:33:30.210
How we secure the
accesses within a project,

00:33:30.210 --> 00:33:32.242
and the segment data
within a project.

00:33:32.242 --> 00:33:33.700
And then also, how
to-- once you've

00:33:33.700 --> 00:33:35.970
gotten to 100,000
projects, how do you

00:33:35.970 --> 00:33:38.574
secure the 100,000
projects you have

00:33:38.574 --> 00:33:40.490
to make sure they're
doing something sensible?

00:33:43.000 --> 00:33:45.150
So one of the things that
we like to do at Google

00:33:45.150 --> 00:33:47.108
is we like to make sure,
again, that everything

00:33:47.108 --> 00:33:51.160
has as few privileges as it
needs to get its job done.

00:33:51.160 --> 00:33:53.740
And so what that means
is, for Google, anyway,

00:33:53.740 --> 00:33:57.110
is that typically services are
given access to sensitive data

00:33:57.110 --> 00:33:58.260
and customer data.

00:33:58.260 --> 00:34:00.780
But the engineers,
the developers

00:34:00.780 --> 00:34:03.570
and reliability engineers don't
actually get any permissions

00:34:03.570 --> 00:34:04.600
to the sensitive data.

00:34:04.600 --> 00:34:08.480
Instead, they're given access
to manage the services.

00:34:08.480 --> 00:34:10.159
And they can, if
they need, then go

00:34:10.159 --> 00:34:11.980
access the data through
the services where

00:34:11.980 --> 00:34:14.139
business logic to secure
and lock down access

00:34:14.139 --> 00:34:16.780
can be done very specifically.

00:34:19.940 --> 00:34:22.520
SUBHASH SANKURATRIPATI:
So I think as Rae already

00:34:22.520 --> 00:34:24.770
mentioned, prior to
the advent of Iam,

00:34:24.770 --> 00:34:26.520
there were pretty
much three roles.

00:34:26.520 --> 00:34:28.239
Editor, viewer, and [INAUDIBLE].

00:34:28.239 --> 00:34:30.980
And editor, viewer and owner.

00:34:30.980 --> 00:34:34.719
So if a project has user
data, pretty much even

00:34:34.719 --> 00:34:36.570
the least privileged
role of viewer

00:34:36.570 --> 00:34:38.466
means nothing from a
security perspective.

00:34:38.466 --> 00:34:40.340
Because once you're a
viewer, you pretty much

00:34:40.340 --> 00:34:42.630
have access to the entire
data in the project, which

00:34:42.630 --> 00:34:44.620
is a huge security risk for us.

00:34:44.620 --> 00:34:48.060
So one can always do
some hacks by basically

00:34:48.060 --> 00:34:50.449
revoking viewer access
to certain buckets

00:34:50.449 --> 00:34:51.550
or to data sets.

00:34:51.550 --> 00:34:53.550
But that still gets unwieldy.

00:34:53.550 --> 00:34:56.699
So thanks to Iam, I think
in the last couple of weeks,

00:34:56.699 --> 00:34:59.200
where it was the
floodgates were relieved,

00:34:59.200 --> 00:35:01.440
and we could actually
start using Iam,

00:35:01.440 --> 00:35:04.245
we've been able to significantly
reduce the number of developers

00:35:04.245 --> 00:35:07.470
that actually have access to
these coarse-grained roles.

00:35:07.470 --> 00:35:10.297
And as Rae already
mentioned, some

00:35:10.297 --> 00:35:12.380
of the roles that help us
a lot is the log viewer,

00:35:12.380 --> 00:35:14.470
because most times,
developers are actually

00:35:14.470 --> 00:35:16.032
just trying to debug problems.

00:35:16.032 --> 00:35:17.490
So they can use
the log viewer role

00:35:17.490 --> 00:35:20.190
to get access to the logs, the
App Engine viewer role, which

00:35:20.190 --> 00:35:23.630
gives them a quick snapshot and
the App Engine service admin

00:35:23.630 --> 00:35:26.120
role which allows them
to do traffic splitting,

00:35:26.120 --> 00:35:27.890
to run some
experiments, and also

00:35:27.890 --> 00:35:30.580
change the version-- the
rollback versions in case there

00:35:30.580 --> 00:35:31.780
are any issues.

00:35:31.780 --> 00:35:34.810
And also, we are pretty excited
of what the future can hold.

00:35:34.810 --> 00:35:37.050
Because with things like
the App Engine deployer,

00:35:37.050 --> 00:35:39.310
we can change some
of our workflows.

00:35:39.310 --> 00:35:41.110
And then once those
workflows are changed,

00:35:41.110 --> 00:35:44.960
I think we have a-- we will
have a much larger reduction

00:35:44.960 --> 00:35:48.430
in the number of developers
that actually have access

00:35:48.430 --> 00:35:51.240
to these coarse-grained roles.

00:35:51.240 --> 00:35:52.280
David?

00:35:52.280 --> 00:35:53.988
DAVID "CACHE" MCWHERTER:
Thanks, Subhash.

00:35:55.670 --> 00:35:58.450
Now, continuing with the
theme of locking down access

00:35:58.450 --> 00:36:00.200
to resources, I wanted
to just talk to you

00:36:00.200 --> 00:36:01.574
on the subject of
micro services,

00:36:01.574 --> 00:36:06.780
especially within
GCE apps in GCP.

00:36:06.780 --> 00:36:09.120
New with Iam, as Rae
mentioned, was the ability

00:36:09.120 --> 00:36:11.790
to create multiple service
accounts on a single project.

00:36:11.790 --> 00:36:13.764
And with this capability,
we can basically

00:36:13.764 --> 00:36:15.180
let each of these
service accounts

00:36:15.180 --> 00:36:17.820
represent one of
our micro services,

00:36:17.820 --> 00:36:20.120
give it access to just
the data it needs,

00:36:20.120 --> 00:36:23.261
and then spin up a fleet of VMs
associated with that service

00:36:23.261 --> 00:36:23.760
account.

00:36:23.760 --> 00:36:26.540
And basically do
that again and again,

00:36:26.540 --> 00:36:28.334
making sure that
each service only

00:36:28.334 --> 00:36:29.500
has access to what it needs.

00:36:32.940 --> 00:36:34.960
Subhash, what do you
think about that?

00:36:34.960 --> 00:36:36.793
SUBHASH SANKURATRIPATI:
So the possibilities

00:36:36.793 --> 00:36:38.830
that this opens up is
pretty much endless.

00:36:38.830 --> 00:36:42.110
So one of the cool
things you can

00:36:42.110 --> 00:36:43.820
do with this is imagine
you have-- you're

00:36:43.820 --> 00:36:47.010
trying to have two micro
services in a given project.

00:36:47.010 --> 00:36:50.610
With the compute engine
service accounts of the past,

00:36:50.610 --> 00:36:55.160
there is no way to
realistically segregate

00:36:55.160 --> 00:36:57.850
the accesses that each of
those micro services has.

00:36:57.850 --> 00:37:01.480
So you could always hack it up
by creating a different service

00:37:01.480 --> 00:37:04.760
account, manually pushing
those service account

00:37:04.760 --> 00:37:08.000
private public keys
to the entire fleet.

00:37:08.000 --> 00:37:11.030
But then, like, anybody
that can log into that box

00:37:11.030 --> 00:37:12.280
has access to the private key.

00:37:12.280 --> 00:37:13.910
It gets pretty messy.

00:37:13.910 --> 00:37:15.870
So imagine this scenario
we're talking about

00:37:15.870 --> 00:37:17.940
like, Alice has her
own micro service.

00:37:17.940 --> 00:37:20.330
Bob has his own micro service.

00:37:20.330 --> 00:37:26.020
Only Alice can deploy code
to her micro service fleet.

00:37:26.020 --> 00:37:28.670
And so Bob can deploy to his.

00:37:28.670 --> 00:37:30.930
So the only persons that
can actually deploy to it

00:37:30.930 --> 00:37:34.290
is the owners of
the service account.

00:37:34.290 --> 00:37:37.200
What this allows you to do,
for example, each micro service

00:37:37.200 --> 00:37:40.170
could have its own bootstrapping
secrets or its own encryption

00:37:40.170 --> 00:37:41.740
keys or data encryption keys.

00:37:41.740 --> 00:37:44.240
They're effectively tied down
to that compute engine service

00:37:44.240 --> 00:37:44.950
account.

00:37:44.950 --> 00:37:47.340
And only the owners
of that micro service

00:37:47.340 --> 00:37:49.180
can actually deploy code to it.

00:37:49.180 --> 00:37:50.930
You can actually take
it one step further,

00:37:50.930 --> 00:37:53.510
where Alice and
Bob don't actually

00:37:53.510 --> 00:37:55.050
have direct access to deploy.

00:37:55.050 --> 00:37:58.010
They actually delegate
that authority

00:37:58.010 --> 00:38:01.260
to a deployer service account,
which then has the authority

00:38:01.260 --> 00:38:05.590
to deploy to the fleet of
micro service VM instances.

00:38:05.590 --> 00:38:08.730
Which means the developers
don't actually have access

00:38:08.730 --> 00:38:13.020
to the raw instances, but
they can always get to it

00:38:13.020 --> 00:38:14.670
in case there's an emergency.

00:38:14.670 --> 00:38:18.710
And this also opens up
the ability to do true

00:38:18.710 --> 00:38:21.710
micro-service-to-micro-service
authentication.

00:38:21.710 --> 00:38:23.970
Imagine you have these
two Compute Engine service

00:38:23.970 --> 00:38:24.660
accounts.

00:38:24.660 --> 00:38:27.780
Nobody but Google
has the private keys.

00:38:27.780 --> 00:38:29.500
Of course, Google is trusted.

00:38:29.500 --> 00:38:32.030
And given in that
paradigm, there

00:38:32.030 --> 00:38:35.720
is nobody else that can
actually sign on that

00:38:35.720 --> 00:38:37.920
or act on behalf of
that service account.

00:38:37.920 --> 00:38:41.190
And when the server receives
it or the target receives it,

00:38:41.190 --> 00:38:44.450
can actually vouch for it that
this is indeed this message

00:38:44.450 --> 00:38:48.190
or GWT has indeed been signed
by the service account.

00:38:48.190 --> 00:38:50.510
And there is an
independent way for me

00:38:50.510 --> 00:38:52.120
to query that public key.

00:38:52.120 --> 00:38:54.610
So you actually can do true
micro-service-to-micro-service

00:38:54.610 --> 00:38:57.760
authentication without private
keys floating all around

00:38:57.760 --> 00:38:58.370
the planet.

00:38:58.370 --> 00:39:00.204
So I think this is
pretty compelling for us.

00:39:00.204 --> 00:39:01.453
DAVID "CACHE" MCWHERTER: Amen.

00:39:01.453 --> 00:39:03.190
And I think one
of the key aspects

00:39:03.190 --> 00:39:06.950
there's like when I'm auditing
my engineer's source code,

00:39:06.950 --> 00:39:09.040
I don't actually have
to care about what

00:39:09.040 --> 00:39:10.580
they're doing with their
private keys anymore, right?

00:39:10.580 --> 00:39:11.860
I don't have to make sure
they're deleting them,

00:39:11.860 --> 00:39:12.490
rotating them.

00:39:12.490 --> 00:39:13.965
Oh, my gosh.

00:39:13.965 --> 00:39:14.465
Huge.

00:39:17.457 --> 00:39:20.040
SUBHASH SANKURATRIPATI: So you
know what else could be cooler?

00:39:24.450 --> 00:39:28.440
The nature or the
content of, as one knows,

00:39:28.440 --> 00:39:30.810
everything that SnapChat
or any content that's

00:39:30.810 --> 00:39:33.340
shared on SnapChat is FMRL.

00:39:33.340 --> 00:39:35.970
So we figured, why not
take it to the next level

00:39:35.970 --> 00:39:39.210
and say the developer
access to critical resources

00:39:39.210 --> 00:39:41.000
is also FMRL?

00:39:41.000 --> 00:39:45.460
And what if, in a steady state
nobody had access to anything?

00:39:45.460 --> 00:39:46.710
DAVID "CACHE" MCWHERTER: What?

00:39:46.710 --> 00:39:48.500
Are you feeling OK, Subhash?

00:39:48.500 --> 00:39:49.230
SUBHASH SANKURATRIPATI:
Hear me out.

00:39:49.230 --> 00:39:50.810
Rae gave us some
new functionality.

00:39:50.810 --> 00:39:52.690
Let's see what it does for us.

00:39:52.690 --> 00:39:54.710
DAVID "CACHE" MCWHERTER: OK.

00:39:54.710 --> 00:39:58.290
SUBHASH SANKURATRIPATI: So we
basically built a ACL leasing

00:39:58.290 --> 00:39:58.915
infrastructure.

00:39:58.915 --> 00:40:01.210
So imagine you have an
access-controlled service that

00:40:01.210 --> 00:40:07.360
has the ability to set an Iam
policy on a set of projects

00:40:07.360 --> 00:40:09.402
or the entire organization.

00:40:09.402 --> 00:40:11.360
Now what you could do
is, supposing an engineer

00:40:11.360 --> 00:40:13.530
wants access to a
resource, the engineer can

00:40:13.530 --> 00:40:17.590
go in to that service,
say, I will need access

00:40:17.590 --> 00:40:19.860
to this resource,
specify a time,

00:40:19.860 --> 00:40:22.380
and then that access-controlled
service can actually

00:40:22.380 --> 00:40:24.985
go back and check
whether that engineer is

00:40:24.985 --> 00:40:27.920
in the whitelisted set of users
for that particular resource

00:40:27.920 --> 00:40:29.090
they're trying to access.

00:40:29.090 --> 00:40:30.970
If yes, they're
given that access

00:40:30.970 --> 00:40:32.570
for that short period of time.

00:40:32.570 --> 00:40:35.900
And then automatically, and
when the duration of the lease

00:40:35.900 --> 00:40:39.160
has expired,
basically the service

00:40:39.160 --> 00:40:43.220
would do another set Iam
policy without that user.

00:40:43.220 --> 00:40:46.300
And the resource,
and boom, the access

00:40:46.300 --> 00:40:48.139
to that resource for
that developer is gone.

00:40:48.139 --> 00:40:49.430
DAVID "CACHE" MCWHERTER: Crazy.

00:40:49.430 --> 00:40:51.460
Only SnapChat would
invent temporary FMRL.

00:40:51.460 --> 00:40:52.960
SUBHASH SANKURATRIPATI:
[INAUDIBLE].

00:40:53.615 --> 00:40:56.240
DAVID "CACHE" MCWHERTER: So now
that we've solved the project--

00:40:56.240 --> 00:40:58.698
Iam has solved the project--
there's the problem of project

00:40:58.698 --> 00:41:00.710
level security, I
was hoping to move on

00:41:00.710 --> 00:41:03.390
to talking about my favorite
topic, organizational level

00:41:03.390 --> 00:41:04.607
security.

00:41:04.607 --> 00:41:06.690
SUBHASH SANKURATRIPATI:
Hang on, hang on, hang on.

00:41:06.690 --> 00:41:07.410
DAVID "CACHE"
MCWHERTER: What's that?

00:41:07.410 --> 00:41:08.900
SUBHASH SANKURATRIPATI:
So Iam has

00:41:08.900 --> 00:41:12.570
allowed us to significantly
improve our access control

00:41:12.570 --> 00:41:13.720
posture.

00:41:13.720 --> 00:41:16.210
But if you look at the
gamut of resources-- just go

00:41:16.210 --> 00:41:18.030
to the developer
console and see there's

00:41:18.030 --> 00:41:21.240
probably 25 different
kinds of resources.

00:41:21.240 --> 00:41:24.477
We've gotten resource--
our roles, for, say,

00:41:24.477 --> 00:41:26.060
about seven or eight
of them-- there's

00:41:26.060 --> 00:41:29.220
a whole gamut of resources
for which we need roles for.

00:41:29.220 --> 00:41:32.180
For example, BigQuery,
DataStore, TaskQueues.

00:41:32.180 --> 00:41:34.939
There's a whole gamut
of them that Rae

00:41:34.939 --> 00:41:35.980
will give us pretty soon.

00:41:35.980 --> 00:41:37.510
She's still going
to be on my number

00:41:37.510 --> 00:41:40.500
one favorites on
Google Hangouts.

00:41:40.500 --> 00:41:42.000
And of course, we
need custom roles.

00:41:42.000 --> 00:41:46.340
Because each organization
always has the need

00:41:46.340 --> 00:41:48.750
for doing something special.

00:41:48.750 --> 00:41:54.160
What if Google allowed us the
ability to say take this role,

00:41:54.160 --> 00:41:56.660
but I don't like this
particular functionality.

00:41:56.660 --> 00:41:58.720
So instead of a customer
being able to create

00:41:58.720 --> 00:42:01.150
or having to create an
entire role from scratch,

00:42:01.150 --> 00:42:03.070
just minor tweaks to it.

00:42:03.070 --> 00:42:05.310
And the next thing that
we're also be able-- we

00:42:05.310 --> 00:42:07.420
have implemented or hacked
together on our own,

00:42:07.420 --> 00:42:09.710
because the DataStore
doesn't actually

00:42:09.710 --> 00:42:14.000
have kind-level permissions or
namespace-level permissions.

00:42:14.000 --> 00:42:15.750
So in the absence of
that, what we've done

00:42:15.750 --> 00:42:17.750
is basically in
remote API, we're

00:42:17.750 --> 00:42:20.246
parsing the proto buffs
as they're coming through.

00:42:20.246 --> 00:42:21.620
We're constructing
these objects,

00:42:21.620 --> 00:42:23.810
extract the namespace
and the kinds.

00:42:23.810 --> 00:42:25.670
And then we're doing
ACLs on top of it.

00:42:25.670 --> 00:42:29.430
So far from ideal, we would
love for Google to solve it.

00:42:29.430 --> 00:42:31.560
And that's one
more thing that I'm

00:42:31.560 --> 00:42:33.720
hoping Rae is going to
give us pretty soon.

00:42:33.720 --> 00:42:34.140
DAVID "CACHE"
MCWHERTER: Good point.

00:42:34.140 --> 00:42:34.940
Good point.

00:42:34.940 --> 00:42:36.547
Thankfully, I'm
sure the Iam team

00:42:36.547 --> 00:42:38.880
will be happy to know they'll
still have jobs next week.

00:42:38.880 --> 00:42:39.610
SUBHASH SANKURATRIPATI:
There's one more thing.

00:42:39.610 --> 00:42:40.220
Sorry.

00:42:40.220 --> 00:42:42.680
So we've got enough
ex-Googlers in our company.

00:42:42.680 --> 00:42:45.040
And any time we talk
anything about authentication

00:42:45.040 --> 00:42:49.730
and authorization, they tell me
the super secure reverse proxy

00:42:49.730 --> 00:42:51.870
that Google has that
apparently solves everything

00:42:51.870 --> 00:42:54.411
on the planet-- I can't say the
name because they'll probably

00:42:54.411 --> 00:42:58.150
kill me-- but let's call it
a secure reverse proxy that

00:42:58.150 --> 00:43:00.960
can effectively perform
any authentication,

00:43:00.960 --> 00:43:03.450
any authorization
decision, and force

00:43:03.450 --> 00:43:05.050
additional levels
of authentication.

00:43:05.050 --> 00:43:06.600
Hey, tap your UB key.

00:43:06.600 --> 00:43:09.460
You haven't tapped it in
more than five minutes.

00:43:09.460 --> 00:43:10.440
Tap it again.

00:43:10.440 --> 00:43:12.550
This is a sensitive
resource, so I'm

00:43:12.550 --> 00:43:15.024
hoping that Google's going to
deliver us that capability.

00:43:15.024 --> 00:43:16.440
DAVID "CACHE"
MCWHERTER: I have it

00:43:16.440 --> 00:43:18.930
on good authority that some
people are working on it.

00:43:18.930 --> 00:43:20.280
These very problems, Subhash.

00:43:20.280 --> 00:43:21.488
SUBHASH SANKURATRIPATI: Cool.

00:43:21.488 --> 00:43:22.720
Thank you.

00:43:22.720 --> 00:43:26.990
DAVID "CACHE" MARKETER: OK, so
now organizational security.

00:43:26.990 --> 00:43:30.790
So at Google, we
have a basic theory

00:43:30.790 --> 00:43:33.290
on organizational security
in that we don't really

00:43:33.290 --> 00:43:35.007
want to get too
involved in managing--

00:43:35.007 --> 00:43:37.090
micromanaging our engineers
in what they're doing.

00:43:37.090 --> 00:43:38.890
We want to give them
generally free leash

00:43:38.890 --> 00:43:41.590
to do what they need to do,
so long as it's reasonable.

00:43:41.590 --> 00:43:45.730
And 99% of the time,
it's irrelevant to Google

00:43:45.730 --> 00:43:47.270
security posture.

00:43:47.270 --> 00:43:49.540
But sometimes, occasionally,
we get situations

00:43:49.540 --> 00:43:51.350
where an application
needs to access

00:43:51.350 --> 00:43:54.034
secure data, company-critical
data, or talk

00:43:54.034 --> 00:43:56.450
to other services that are
fairly important to the company

00:43:56.450 --> 00:43:57.144
as a whole.

00:43:57.144 --> 00:43:59.310
And that's where my team
tends to get more involved.

00:44:04.450 --> 00:44:07.640
From my perspective, I think one
of the biggest things that this

00:44:07.640 --> 00:44:09.950
is basically rooted in is
understanding what's going

00:44:09.950 --> 00:44:12.980
on inside the company from
like a 20,000-foot level.

00:44:12.980 --> 00:44:16.440
And so the org node
policies that we can apply

00:44:16.440 --> 00:44:18.520
will let us take
actually snapshots

00:44:18.520 --> 00:44:24.050
of what's going on in all the
Google-owned GCP projects.

00:44:24.050 --> 00:44:27.940
And that's like an
incredibly powerful tool.

00:44:27.940 --> 00:44:29.749
Then after we know
what's going on,

00:44:29.749 --> 00:44:31.290
then we can start
reasoning about it.

00:44:31.290 --> 00:44:33.400
And then we can then
try to make sure

00:44:33.400 --> 00:44:35.870
that only the right kinds
of things are happening,

00:44:35.870 --> 00:44:37.799
either by talking
to the engineers who

00:44:37.799 --> 00:44:39.840
are creating the projects
and using the projects,

00:44:39.840 --> 00:44:41.591
or by actually going
in organizationally

00:44:41.591 --> 00:44:43.590
and shutting things down
that shouldn't be done,

00:44:43.590 --> 00:44:44.590
shouldn't be allowed.

00:44:44.590 --> 00:44:47.059
And what's super important to
me-- I assume also to you--

00:44:47.059 --> 00:44:49.350
is that I don't actually
get-- my team doesn't actually

00:44:49.350 --> 00:44:52.540
need permission to go and
read any of the critical date

00:44:52.540 --> 00:44:54.059
or sensitive of the company.

00:44:54.059 --> 00:44:55.600
We really just want
to be able to get

00:44:55.600 --> 00:44:58.600
a sense of what's
going on in the control

00:44:58.600 --> 00:44:59.777
plane of the system.

00:44:59.777 --> 00:45:01.360
SUBHASH SANKURATRIPATI:
Largely agree.

00:45:01.360 --> 00:45:03.230
But I think the one
area, because we

00:45:03.230 --> 00:45:06.390
have a lot of user
data in our projects.

00:45:06.390 --> 00:45:11.270
Anytime a project has user data,
pretty much, the security team

00:45:11.270 --> 00:45:13.760
kind of decides what they
need, because developers always

00:45:13.760 --> 00:45:15.260
want access to everything.

00:45:15.260 --> 00:45:18.740
So the security team will
say, hang on, not so much.

00:45:18.740 --> 00:45:19.370
Let's tweak it.

00:45:19.370 --> 00:45:20.090
DAVID "CACHE"
MCWHERTER: Identify

00:45:20.090 --> 00:45:21.760
the nature of the
application and then

00:45:21.760 --> 00:45:24.910
decide what security
policy should be applied.

00:45:24.910 --> 00:45:25.470
Good point.

00:45:25.470 --> 00:45:28.540
Thankfully, Iam was
designed with these kinds

00:45:28.540 --> 00:45:29.429
of issues in mind.

00:45:29.429 --> 00:45:31.720
And it allows us to do all
sorts of interesting things.

00:45:31.720 --> 00:45:35.000
It allows my team to build
interesting tools to actually

00:45:35.000 --> 00:45:37.227
try to manage our problems.

00:45:37.227 --> 00:45:39.310
And so one of the things
that we've done at Google

00:45:39.310 --> 00:45:41.810
is we've introduced an
onboarding workflow.

00:45:41.810 --> 00:45:44.100
So when new projects are
created by our engineers,

00:45:44.100 --> 00:45:45.900
we can actually discover
them by listening

00:45:45.900 --> 00:45:48.300
at the organizational level.

00:45:48.300 --> 00:45:51.610
And we can basically see who the
people are creating and using

00:45:51.610 --> 00:45:53.870
the project, and we can
send them an email saying,

00:45:53.870 --> 00:45:56.410
by the way, these are the
best practices for security,

00:45:56.410 --> 00:45:58.180
and these are the expectations.

00:45:58.180 --> 00:46:01.280
And this is how you register
for further security reviews,

00:46:01.280 --> 00:46:02.380
if that's necessary.

00:46:02.380 --> 00:46:03.838
This is how you
actually go and get

00:46:03.838 --> 00:46:06.950
access to critical resources,
et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

00:46:06.950 --> 00:46:09.040
Another thing that we've
recently started doing

00:46:09.040 --> 00:46:12.740
is doing a continuous scan
of all the virtual machines

00:46:12.740 --> 00:46:16.050
and all the resources in
use, and actually sending

00:46:16.050 --> 00:46:18.490
those teams a monthly reminder
indicating what they're

00:46:18.490 --> 00:46:19.994
doing, what they're using.

00:46:19.994 --> 00:46:21.410
And if there's
anything that looks

00:46:21.410 --> 00:46:22.930
like it could be
terminated, like VMs that

00:46:22.930 --> 00:46:25.060
could be shut down, we
give them the suggestion

00:46:25.060 --> 00:46:26.110
to turn them off.

00:46:26.110 --> 00:46:29.088
And the best-- most
secure VM is a dead VM.

00:46:29.088 --> 00:46:30.514
If you ask me.

00:46:30.514 --> 00:46:31.680
SUBHASH SANKURATRIPATI: So--

00:46:31.680 --> 00:46:33.020
DAVID "CACHE" MCWHERTER:
What do you think?

00:46:33.020 --> 00:46:33.320
SUBHASH SANKURATRIPATI:
I think it's

00:46:33.320 --> 00:46:35.680
great we've pretty much
followed the same philosophy,

00:46:35.680 --> 00:46:37.310
except in cases
where, of course,

00:46:37.310 --> 00:46:39.880
as I said earlier,
project that use the data.

00:46:39.880 --> 00:46:42.830
And one example I want
to give is recently,

00:46:42.830 --> 00:46:45.120
there was that March 1
open SSL vulnerability

00:46:45.120 --> 00:46:48.300
was going to be-- vulnerability
was going to be released.

00:46:48.300 --> 00:46:51.580
So you're like, OK,
what is our footprint?

00:46:51.580 --> 00:46:53.580
Prior to the
organization umbrella,

00:46:53.580 --> 00:46:55.610
we would have no
way of knowing it.

00:46:55.610 --> 00:46:57.060
Thankfully, Rae whitelisted us.

00:46:57.060 --> 00:46:58.560
We were on the alpha program.

00:46:58.560 --> 00:47:03.040
So we went, got assigned a
compute instance admin role

00:47:03.040 --> 00:47:04.980
to a service account
team, made sure

00:47:04.980 --> 00:47:06.790
that you're able
to get an accurate

00:47:06.790 --> 00:47:09.330
inventory of every
virtual machine we owned.

00:47:09.330 --> 00:47:12.720
And then populated the
keys also just to make sure

00:47:12.720 --> 00:47:14.570
that in case it was Heartbleed.

00:47:14.570 --> 00:47:17.530
Next, we had the ability
to quickly go and patch.

00:47:17.530 --> 00:47:18.780
Thankfully, we didn't have to.

00:47:18.780 --> 00:47:21.420
But the fact that
the org node existed

00:47:21.420 --> 00:47:23.810
enabled us to actually
have the assurance

00:47:23.810 --> 00:47:25.149
that we know what we own.

00:47:25.149 --> 00:47:26.690
DAVID "CACHE"
MCWHERTER: Great story.

00:47:26.690 --> 00:47:30.530
And now you guys can
do the same thing.

00:47:30.530 --> 00:47:34.310
So another thing that
Google likes to do,

00:47:34.310 --> 00:47:36.470
Subhash, is that
pretty much everything

00:47:36.470 --> 00:47:39.040
that we run in
production tends to go

00:47:39.040 --> 00:47:42.810
through a secure-- not a secure,
but a source code review--

00:47:42.810 --> 00:47:45.570
and then gets committed to
our source control system.

00:47:45.570 --> 00:47:47.520
And then from there,
only from there

00:47:47.520 --> 00:47:49.150
does it get pushed
to production.

00:47:49.150 --> 00:47:52.504
And previously, before
we had all the APIs

00:47:52.504 --> 00:47:54.920
we now have from Iam, it was
actually kind of a little bit

00:47:54.920 --> 00:47:55.420
hard.

00:47:55.420 --> 00:47:57.330
We couldn't actually
ensure that.

00:47:57.330 --> 00:47:59.610
All the policy updates
had to be done by hand.

00:47:59.610 --> 00:48:01.990
But now we have a way of
programmatically managing

00:48:01.990 --> 00:48:03.450
ACLs and other things.

00:48:03.450 --> 00:48:05.420
So we can actually
write tooling that

